## 13. THE 20-CRITERIA INVESTMENT RUBRIC

### Scoring Scale

| Level | Label | Meaning |
|-------|-------|---------|
| **1** | Value Destructive | Irrational expectations, destructive economics, behavioral red flags → **Strong Sell** |
| **2** | Weak | Below-average economics, inside-view bias, unfavorable odds → **Sell / Avoid** |
| **3** | Neutral / Priced-in | Status quo, earning cost of capital, standard management → **Hold** |
| **4** | Strong | Favorable asymmetry, positive return spreads, rational allocation → **Buy** |
| **5** | Exemplary | Massive expectation gap, outstanding moat, elite cognitive flexibility → **Strong Buy** |

### I. Expectations & Valuation

| # | Criterion | Level 1 | Level 3 | Level 5 |
|---|-----------|---------|---------|---------|
| 1 | **Price-Implied Expectations (PIE)** | Heroic, unprecedented growth; defies historical reality | Accurately reflects consensus base case | Dismal bar with highly favorable fundamentals → massive expectation gap |
| 2 | **Base Rate Calibration** | 0% historical base rate of success | Aligns with median base rates | Market pricing extreme downward mean-reversion; base rates say steady outperformance likely |
| 3 | **Market-Implied CAP (MICAP)** | 20+ year CAP for rapidly changing, competitive firm | Matches realistic moat duration | Market assumes near-zero CAP for business with enduring, high-return moats |
| 4 | **Expected Value & Babe Ruth Effect** | Guaranteed negative EV; catastrophic fat-tail downside (blowup) | Balanced; upside and downside symmetrical | Extreme positive EV (bleed strategy); massive slugging ratio compensates low batting average |
| 5 | **Market Diversity & Sentiment** | Complete diversity breakdown (euphoria); meme speculation | Efficient pricing; independent information processing | Total diversity breakdown (panic); universally pessimistic → massive mispricing |

### II. Economic Reality & Cash Flow

| # | Criterion | Level 1 | Level 3 | Level 5 |
|---|-----------|---------|---------|---------|
| 6 | **ROIC vs. WACC** | ROIC persistently below WACC; value destruction | ROIC = WACC; value-neutral steady state | ROIC massively exceeds WACC; exceptional capital efficiency |
| 7 | **Growth Value Amplification** | Rapid growth with negative ROIC spread (accelerating destruction) | Modest growth, value-neutral | Explosive growth on highly positive ROIC; massively amplifying intrinsic value |
| 8 | **Intangibles Adjustment** | Non-GAAP metrics hide cash burn (SBC add-backs) | Standard tangible business; GAAP reflects reality | "GAAP Losers" — heavy intangible investment suppresses accounting profit while building massive future value |
| 9 | **Capital Intensity & CCC** | Ballooning positive CCC; growth drains cash | Standard working capital needs | Negative CCC; suppliers finance growth (e.g., Amazon -33 days) |
| 10 | **Real Options & Volatility** | Rigid irreversible investments in declining low-volatility industry | Stable business with predictable cash flows | High volatility + exceptional mgmt + abundant capital = massive unpriced strategic optionality |

### III. Competitive Strategy & Moat

| # | Criterion | Level 1 | Level 3 | Level 5 |
|---|-----------|---------|---------|---------|
| 11 | **Barrier to Entry Durability** | Zero barriers; commodity product | Standard industry barriers | Impregnable moats — economies of scale + network effects |
| 12 | **Strategic Positioning** | Stuck in the middle | Functional competitor, no distinct advantage | Elite differentiator with massive pricing power and unassailable brand/IP |
| 13 | **Willingness to Pay** | Value-extracting monopoly; fragile to disruption | Standard pricing power | Relentlessly creates massive consumer surplus; unbreakable customer lock-in |
| 14 | **Intangible Scalability** | Massive sunk costs in obsolete intangibles, zero salvage value | Balanced asset base | Elite "recombination" engine; mixing digital building blocks to create hyper-dominant platforms |
| 15 | **Industry Lifecycle** | Decline stage; generating cash but bleeding share | Mature consolidation; stable but unexciting | Industry consolidation complete; surviving apex predator reaping oligopolistic spoils |

### IV. Capital Allocation & Governance

| # | Criterion | Level 1 | Level 3 | Level 5 |
|---|-----------|---------|---------|---------|
| 16 | **M&A Discipline** | Transformational empire-building with expensive stock; EPS accretion fallacy | Halting M&A; returning capital safely | NPV of synergies vastly exceeds premium paid; elite M&A team |
| 17 | **Share Repurchase Rationale** | Buying overvalued stock to hit EPS targets or offset SBC dilution | Fair value; consistent buybacks to return cash | Intrinsic Value School; massive buybacks only at deep discounts when reinvestment exhausted |
| 18 | **R&D Efficiency (RQ)** | Plummeting productivity; products obsolete before launch | R&D matches industry maintenance requirements | Elite RQ; every 1% R&D increase generates disproportionate revenue surge |
| 19 | **Management Incentives** | Sacrificing long-term NPV to hit quarterly EPS | Standard compensation; vulnerable to peer-mimicking | Tied directly to long-term per-share value and ROIC improvement |
| 20 | **Zero-Based Capital Allocation** | Extreme inertia; budgets based on last year's politics | Mildly optimizing; slow to reallocate | True zero-based; every division re-justifies annually; starves bad growth to fund optimal strategies |

