## 14. FAILURE MODES & BLIND SPOTS

Mauboussin explicitly acknowledges these limitations to his framework:

### 14.1 The Base Rate Blind Spot

Strict adherence to base rates causes investors to miss massive, world-changing outliers. If Elon Musk strictly obeyed base rates, he would never attempt his ventures.

**Example:** Applying historical base rates to OpenAI makes their projected 108% CAGR look like a statistical impossibility (0 out of 18,900 public companies achieved it). Yet they are currently outpacing it.

**Resolution:** Base rates are the prior. Exceptional companies can update that prior. But the base case must never be the outlier scenario.

### 14.2 Intangible Asset-Light Illusions

Capitalizing intangibles reveals these businesses have **wider standard deviations** — they produce more mega-winners, but also more spectacular bankruptcies (Enron) with zero salvage value. Asset-light ≠ inherently safer.

### 14.3 Personal Forecasting Errors

Mauboussin admitted he was wrong for a full year on Nvidia and semiconductor fragility, underestimating the staying power of their model despite the threat of customers building custom silicon.

### 14.4 Reconciling Active Management vs. Efficient Markets

**The line is drawn at:**

1. **Systemic diversity breakdowns** — when cognitive diversity collapses, massive gaps between price and value open up
2. **High dispersion environments** — active research only makes sense where winner-loser spread is wide (VC, small-cap, micro-cap)
3. **Short-term vs. long-term arbitrage** — the market can be efficient in the short term while creating massive opportunities for patient capital

When no inefficiency is visible: buy the index. When inefficiency appears: deploy concentrated capital.

